message.content:
Below is a **complete, self‑contained Java 8 example** that shows how to:

1. Take a `char[]` called `textBuffer`.  
2. Extract a *substring* that starts at index 5 and contains 10 characters.  
3. Escape those characters so that they are safe for XML.  
4. Append the escaped text to an output stream (e.g., a `StringBuilder`, a `Writer`, or any other “text stream”).  
5. Call the supplied helper `functions.ToTextStream_characters` to actually write the characters.

---

## 1.  What the helper does

```java
// Provided by the platform – you do NOT implement this yourself.
namespace functions {
    type ToTextStream_characters = (_: {
        ch: string,      // the character array (as a Java array)
        start: string,   // start index (int)
        length: string   // number of chars to write (int)
    }) => any;
}
```

> **Important** – The helper expects the *Java array* (`char[]`) itself, **not** a `String`.  
> The wrapper around the helper will convert the Java types into the string‑based representation that the platform understands.

---

## 2.  Escaping for XML

XML has four characters that must be escaped when they appear in text content:

| Character | Escape sequence |
|-----------|-----------------|
| `&`       | `&amp;`         |
| `<`       | `&lt;`          |
| `>`       | `&gt;`          |
| `"`       | `&quot;`        |
| `'`       | `&apos;`        |

You can either use Apache Commons Text (`StringEscapeUtils.escapeXml10`) or write a tiny helper yourself.  
Below we use a hand‑rolled method because it keeps the example completely self‑contained.

```java
/**
 * Escapes the characters that are special in XML.
 * @param src the source character array
 * @param start the start index
 * @param length the number of characters to escape
 * @return a String containing the escaped text
 */
private static String escapeXml(char[] src, int start, int length) {
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(length * 2); // worst case: every char escapes
    for (int i = start; i < start + length; i++) {
        char c = src[i];
        switch (c) {
            case '&': sb.append("&amp;"); break;
            case '<': sb.append("&lt;");  break;
            case '>': sb.append("&gt;");  break;
            case '\"': sb.append("&quot;"); break;
            case '\'': sb.append("&apos;"); break;
            default: sb.append(c);
        }
    }
    return sb.toString();
}
```

---

## 3.  Full example

```java
import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.Writer;
import java.io.StringWriter;

public class XmlAppendExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        // 1. Prepare a sample text buffer
        String sample = "Hello, this is a sample text buffer used for demonstration.";
        char[] textBuffer = sample.toCharArray();

        // 2. Define the substring parameters
        int startIndex = 5;   // start at the 6th character (index 5)
        int subLength  = 10;  // take 10 characters

        // 3. Validate bounds
        if (startIndex < 0 || subLength < 0 ||
            startIndex + subLength > textBuffer.length) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Substring out of bounds");
        }

        // 4. Escape the substring for XML
        String escaped = escapeXml(textBuffer, startIndex, subLength);

        // 5. Write the escaped text to a Writer (you could also use a StringBuilder)
        Writer out = new StringWriter();   // replace with your actual output stream
        out.write(escaped);
        out.flush();

        // 6. If you need to use the platform helper, call it like this:
        //    (the helper will internally write to the same stream you provide)
        //    functions.ToTextStream_characters({ch: textBuffer, start: startIndex, length: subLength});
        //    // Note: the helper writes raw characters – it does NOT perform XML escaping.
        //    // Therefore you must escape first (as we did above) before calling the helper
        //    // if you want the output to be XML‑safe.

        // 7. Print the result to verify
        System.out.println("Escaped XML fragment:");
        System.out.println(out.toString());
    }

    // --- escapeXml() implementation from section 2 ---
    private static String escapeXml(char[] src, int start, int length) {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(length * 2);
        for (int i = start; i < start + length; i++) {
            char c = src[i];
            switch (c) {
                case '&': sb.append("&amp;"); break;
                case '<': sb.append("&lt;");  break;
                case '>': sb.append("&gt;");  break;
                case '\"': sb.append("&quot;"); break;
                case '\'': sb.append("&apos;"); break;
                default: sb.append(c);
            }
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }
}
```

### What the program prints

```
Escaped XML fragment:
, this is 
```

*(The comma is part of the original text; the substring “, this is ” is exactly 10 characters long.)*

---

## 4.  How to integrate with the platform helper

If you *must* use the platform’s `functions.ToTextStream_characters` helper (for example, because the platform requires it for certain serialization pipelines), you can do so **after** you have escaped the text:

```java
// 1. Escape the substring
String escaped = escapeXml(textBuffer, startIndex, subLength);

// 2. Convert the escaped string back to a char array
char[] escapedChars = escaped.toCharArray();

// 3. Call the helper
functions.ToTextStream_characters({
    ch: escapedChars,
    start: 0,
    length: escapedChars.length
});
```

> **Why do we convert back to a `char[]`?**  
> The helper works on raw character arrays, not on `String`s.  
> By converting the escaped string back to a `char[]`, we let the helper write the exact escaped text to the underlying stream.

---

## 5.  Summary

| Step | What to do | Why |
|------|------------|-----|
| 1 | Extract the desired slice from `textBuffer`. | You only want a specific portion. |
| 2 | Escape the slice for XML. | Prevents malformed XML. |
| 3 | Write the escaped slice to your output stream. | Produces the final serialized XML. |
| 4 | (Optional) Call `functions.ToTextStream_characters` with the escaped slice. | Required if the platform mandates that helper. |

Feel free to copy the code into your project, adjust the `Writer` to whatever output you need (e.g., a `FileWriter`, `OutputStreamWriter`, etc.), and you’re good to go!
